  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1219 AM CST Fri Nov 13 2026\n\nUpper trough/low over the eastern CONUS with a weak ridge aloft \nacross the eastern Rockies into the High Plains to start off the \nforecast for today. Northerly flow aloft directing cooler air into \nthe region, with a west to east thermal gradient across the \nDakotas/Minnesota. As high pressure makes a slow retreat to the \neast, increased southeasterlies thanks to about 14mb gradient across \nthe state. That gets to about 20mb by Saturday morning, so winds to \nremain up tonight. This also means that upslope flow stratus in the \nfar western CWA also to remain in place. \n\nFor Saturday night/Sunday, a trough coming in across the Rockies but \nis in the process of weakening. This appears to be a split flow type \nsituation, with the primary wave within this flow regime moving \nacross the Desert Southwest/Southern Plains, with another wave \nmoving across southern/central Canada. Thus, limited weather impact \nwith this feature aside from just a slight weakening of the \ngradient, down to around 12-14mb across the state, although oriented \nmore southwest to northeast and thus winds out of the due south. Not \na massive or substantial warmup aloft, but milder for Sunday with \n850mb temperatures up to around +4 to +8C... which is approaching a \nstandard deviation above climo. NBM temperatures are topping out \naround 10 to 15F above climo in response. \n\nSo the weakening upper trough moves overhead late in the \nday/overnight Sunday so little more than some scattered mid/high \nclouds. Without a surface reflection, low level flow remains \npersistent into Monday/Tuesday. Peak 'heat' is supposedly Monday, \nwith a slight decrease in temperatures aloft Tuesday with a \nsouthwest flow shortwave lifting out ahead of the next broader upper \nwave, but moisture with this feature is up around 200-300mb and \n850mb temperatures are still a standard deviation above climo.  \n\nFocus shifts to a low that moves across the area Wednesday as the \nupper trough likewise lifts up across the area. With the moisture on \nthe northern and western portions of the low, focus area for most \nQPF is up across North Dakota.  The lower ranges of ensembles is \ndown around zero for moisture, with the 75th percentile up around \n1/4 to 1/3 of an inch along the ND/SD state line. Thus, a \nslight wiggle southwards would slightly improve our chances for\nmoisture.",
